0.2, "rationale": "Expectations anchored and fresh outweighs hot-but-fading realised inflation; net signal too weak/offsetting to warrant a directional breakeven bet."}}

{"notes": "The wedge here is a textbook case of realised inflation running hot while expectations stay anchored. Headline CPI has accelerated sharply (6.90% y/y, 3m annualized 9.48%, core PCE 4.44%) and inflation's analyst has high conviction and fresh data. But the breakeven analyst, working directly on the 10y breakeven itself, sees the level drifting lower (2.73%→2.50% over three months, -7bp over 21 days) and interprets this as re-anchoring toward the 2% target rather than de-anchoring — a flat call at moderate conviction, freshly formed. Per the instructed hierarchy, when realised inflation is hot but expectations stay anchored, resolve toward the expectations driver, not a split. That is exactly this case: the market's own pricing of forward inflation compensation is not confirming the hot CPI prints, and breakevens are the direct, most relevant measure of the wedge itself — more relevant than CPI backward-looking prints.

The labor tightness read (unemployment falling to 4.10%, Sahm gap turning marginally positive) suggests the tightening cycle is topping out — consistent with the idea that wage-driven inflation persistence may be peaking, reinforcing the anchored-expectations case rather than an accelerating-inflation-expectations case. This is a supportive cross-read for the expectations analyst's flat call, not a reason to override it.

I do not have a fresh macro reason to flip the expectations call to down given its own falsifier (sub-2.35%) hasn't been breached, and its analyst explicitly frames this as mean-reversion/re-anchoring, not a breakout lower. So my net view is: hot realised inflation exists, but market-priced expectations are flat-to-very-slightly softer, and per the resolution rule this dominates. I lean toward flat-to-mild-narrow on breakevens, not a widening bet, despite the loud realised-inflation print.", "drivers": [{"driver": "inflation", "conviction": 0.68, "why": "Passed through analyst's own hot CPI/core PCE reading; realised inflation itself is clearly rising, high conviction, but subordinated to expectations for the wedge call."}, {"driver": "inflation_expectations", "conviction": -0.15, "why": "Breakeven drifting down over 3 months/21 days, framed as re-anchoring toward target; flat-to-slightly-lower is the operative signal for the wedge."}, {"driver": "labor_tightness", "conviction": 0.42, "why": "Unemployment rate's own reading still falling per analyst's headline call, but Sahm gap turning positive flags tightening cycle topping — used only as cross-read support for anchored expectations, not a driver of its own view here."}], "leaned_on": ["inflation_expectations", "labor_tightness"], "discounted": [{"driver": "inflation", "why": "Hot and high-conviction, but per resolution rule realised inflation is subordinated to anchored market-priced expectations for the breakeven wedge."}], "falsifier": "Breakeven sustains a move below 2.35% (confirming de-anchoring lower) or above 2.65% (confirming inflation expectations catching up to hot CPI) — either would override this flat/narrow lean.", "confidence": 0.5, "risks": [{"tag": "policy", "text": "Fed could shift language acknowledging persistent inflation, causing expectations to catch up to realised CPI, widening breakevens."}, {"tag": "positioning", "text": "Breakeven analyst's flat call has only moderate conviction (0.45); a fresh data point could quickly flip the read."}, {"tag": "carry", "text": "If hot CPI persists another cycle, market-priced expectations may lag but eventually re-price higher, reversing this narrow lean."}], "trade": {"legs": [{"instrument": "T10YIE", "weight": -0.2}], "conviction": 0.35, "rationale": "Modest narrowing bet: market-priced expectations are drifting lower/anchored despite hot realised inflation; resolve wedge toward expectations per doctrine, but low conviction given proximity to flat."}}

{"notes": "The wedge question is: do 10y breakevens widen or narrow? Realised inflation (headline CPI 7.17% y/y, 3m annualized 10.5%, core PCE 4.89%) is hot and accelerating, formed 16 days ago — moderately fresh. Inflation expectations, formed at this meeting (freshest read), also point up: 10y breakeven at 2.56%, +12bp/21d and +19bp/63d, a new local high, with policy language still anchoring to 2% target and no re-anchoring signal. This is not the classic divergence case (hot realised inflation vs anchored expectations) — here both drivers agree on direction, which raises conviction rather than forcing a resolution toward one driver alone. Per the playbook, expectations get primacy when the two diverge; here they don't diverge, so I weight expectations as the leading, freshest signal, with realised inflation corroborating and providing the underlying persistence story (core-vs-headline both rising, "supply and demand imbalances" language suggesting persistence not transitory framing).

The labor cross-read matters for interpreting whether the inflation persistence is demand-driven and self-reinforcing: unemployment at 3.9%, a 12-month low, below the natural rate estimate, with Sahm gap trivial. Tight labor markets support the case that the inflation acceleration is not merely supply-side transitory but has a demand/wage impulse likely to sustain expectations reappraisal upward, reinforcing the breakeven uptrend rather than contradicting it.

