s, and tight labor market all align toward widening breakevens; moderate conviction sizing given sub-extreme range position."}}

{"notes": "The wedge question is: does realised inflation running hot translate into wider 10y breakevens, or does it stay contained because expectations are anchored? Realised inflation (headline 7.56% y/y, core PCE 5.21% y/y, both accelerating on 3m-annualized basis) is unambiguously hot and the analyst's own conviction (0.80 up) is well-supported by momentum in the data. But this analyst was given no wage/labor cross-read directly — the labor_tightness analyst reports unemployment at 4.0%, exactly at the estimated natural rate, tightening over 12m but decelerating monthly (flat call, 0.70 conviction on the level itself). A labor market at the natural rate with flattening momentum is consistent with wage pressure that is present but not accelerating sharply — it neither confirms nor denies a wage-price spiral. This is a modest cross-read that inflation persistence has some structural underpinning (tight labor) but isn't obviously accelerating from labor alone.

The critical read is inflation_expectations: the 10y breakeven at 2.44% is essentially flat, having pulled back from a "would be up" call to "flat" as the 21d and 63d changes turned negative and the breakeven sits right at the analyst's own falsifier boundary. This is the signature disconnect: realised inflation surging while the market-priced expectations measure stalls or drifts down at the margin. Per the meta-instruction, when realised inflation is hot but expectations stay anchored, we resolve toward the expectations driver rather than splitting the difference or averaging. The expectations analyst is fresh (formed this meeting) and directly measures the breakeven itself, so no staleness discount applies — but the low conviction (0.45) and the flat call itself signal genuine equipoise, tilting slightly down given the 21d/63d momentum is negative even if the longer 252d trend is up.

Net: I lean toward the expectations read dominating — breakevens are more likely to be roughly flat-to-slightly-narrower than to widen sharply, despite the hot realised inflation, because the market's forward-looking gauge is not confirming the print-level acceleration.", "drivers": [{"driver": "inflation", "conviction": 0.75, "why": "Headline and core inflation both accelerating on 3m-annualized basis; momentum unambiguous, no deceleration signal in data."}, {"driver": "inflation_expectations", "conviction": -0.15, "why": "Breakeven at 2.44%, negative 21d/63d momentum, sitting at analyst's own falsifier boundary; resolved toward this over hot realised print."}, {"driver": "labor_tightness", "conviction": -0.1, "why": "Unemployment at natural rate, tightening trend decelerating; modest cross-read of persistent but not accelerating wage pressure."}], "leaned_on": ["inflation_expectations", "inflation"], "discounted": [{"driver": "labor_tightness", "why": "Only an indirect cross-read on inflation persistence; not decisive enough to move the wedge view alone."}], "falsifier": "Breakeven sustaining above 2.48% for two+ weeks, or a hot CPI print paired with expectations survey re-acceleration, would flip this to widening.", "confidence": 0.55, "risks": [{"tag": "policy", "text": "Fed QE resumption could mechanically compress real yields and push breakevens wider via duration demand, not inflation view.", "tag_dup": false}, {"tag": "carry", "text": "Persistent hot CPI prints could eventually force expectations to catch up, widening breakevens despite current anchoring."}, {"tag": "liquidity", "text": "Elevated VIX and stretched positioning could distort breakeven pricing independent of true inflation views."}], "trade": {"legs": [{"instrument": "T10YIE", "weight": -0.3}], "conviction": 0.4, "rationale": "Lean toward narrower breakevens: expectations analyst's flat-to-soft read dominates the hot but unconfirmed realised inflation signal, per resolution rule favoring anchored expectations."}}

{"notes": "This is the textbook divergence case: realised inflation is hot (headline CPI at 7.94% y/y, 3m annualized 8.25%, core PCE 5.36%) while the question for breakevens is whether expectations are re-anchoring higher or the anchor holds. The expectations analyst reports breakeven_10y at 2.62%, up 22bp in 21 days and now at the top of its 12-month range, having breached its own prior falsifier (sustained below 2.40%). That is a genuine, fresh, formed-at-this-meeting signal — not stale — and it is directionally aligned with the inflation print rather than contradicting it. Per the resolution rule, when realised and expectations agree, there's no need to override toward anchoring; the expectations reading itself is already showing de-anchoring pressure, and it is the more directly relevant driver for breakevens since breakevens are by construction a market-priced expectations wedge.

The labor_tightness read is relevant as a cross-check on inflation persistence: unemployment at 3.90%, below the estimated natural rate, with the analyst characterizing the regime as "tight, not equilibrium" despite calling the headline number flat. This tightness supports the persistence case for inflation and indirectly reinforces the expectations move — wage pressure from a taut labor market is a classic channel keeping inflation expectations elevated rather than mean-reverting. This is not a case where I need to override the expectations call with the labor cross-read; instead the labor cross-read corroborates it.

Net: I read breakevens as widening over the horizon. The move is consistent across realised inflation, the expectations market itself, and the labor-tightness backdrop that would sustain it. Conviction is good but not extreme given the expectations analyst's own caveat that the 21-day move could reflect tactical repricing from a trough rather than structural de-anchoring, and the longer 63-day change (+7bp) is much more modest than the 21-day move.", "drivers": [{"driver": "inflation", "conviction": 0.7, "why": "Headline CPI 12m high, 3m annualized 8.25%, core PCE accelerating to 5.36%; momentum intact despite one softer m/m print."}, {"driver": "inflation_expectations", "conviction": 0.6, "why": "Breakeven at 2.62%, +22bp in 21d, prior falsifier breached; genuine fresh signal, tempered by modest 63d change and tactical-repricing caveat."}, {"driver": "labor_tightness", "conviction": -0.1, "why": "Headline unrate flat at 3.90%, but below natural rate—used as cross-read supporting inflation persistence, not a standalone directional call."}], "leaned_on": ["inflation_expectations", "inflation", "labor_tightness"], "discounted": [{"driver": "labor_tightness", "why": "Own headline call is 'flat'; used only as corroborating context for persistence, not as an independent breakeven driver."}], "falsifier": "Breakeven_10y falls back below 2.50% within two weeks, or next CPI m/m drops to 0.35% or below, confirming the move was tactical, not structural.", "confidence": 0.62, "risks": [{"tag": "positioning", "text": "Sharp 21-day breakeven move could be technical/positioning-driven repricing from a trough rather than a durable re-anchoring."}, {"tag": "policy", "text": "Fed language remains generic on inflation tolerance; no explicit policy validation of higher expectations regime yet."}, {"tag": "carry", "text": "Longer 63-day and 252-day breakeven momentum is much softer than the 21-day spike, raising mean-reversion risk."}], "trade": {"legs": [{"instrument": "T10YIE", "weight": 0.55}], "conviction": 0.6, "rationale": "Realised inflation hot, expectations breaking out of range, labor tightness supports persistence — long breakevens on a widening wedge, sized moderately given tactical-repricing risk."}}

{"notes": "The wedge here is defined by a clear divergence between realised inflation (hot, accelerating) and inflation expectations (freshly rolling over). Per the resolution rule, when realised inflation is hot but expectations stay anchored — or here, actively de-anchoring lower — the read should resolve toward the expectations driver, not split the difference. The inflation analyst reports headline CPI accelerating to 8.57% y/y with a blistering 1.12% m/m print and core PCE at 5.60% y/y, all clearly hot and above threshold. But this call was formed 16 days ago and reflects trailing momentum, not the forward-looking price the breakeven market is already setting. The inflation_expectations analyst, formed at this meeting (fresher), shows the 10y breakeven declining 0.11pp over 21 days to 2.84%, reversing an earlier uptrend and approaching territory that would falsify a structural re-anchoring thesis. This is the signature case explicitly flagged in the instructions: hot realised inflation with expectations rolling over should resolve toward expectations, not realised.

I also checked the labor cross-read since inflation persistence claims should be weighed against wage/labor tightness. Labor tightness is up (unemployment falling to 3.70%, below its natural rate estimate), which argues for continued inflationary persistence from a demand-pull, wage-pressure channel — this modestly tempers how much I lean into the expectations-driven narrowing, but does not overturn it, since labor tightness is a slower-moving structural signal and the breakeven has already begun pricing lower despite tight labor.

