ut-hot realised inflation print, tempered by tight labor market persistence risk."}}

{"notes": "The wedge here is between a realised-inflation analyst calling headline CPI modestly up (0.45 conviction, but explicitly citing core PCE deceleration and framing the headline pop as energy/food driven rather than broad-based) and an expectations analyst calling the 10y breakeven down with 0.52 conviction, fresh (formed at this meeting), citing a decisive 24bp/21-day decline, stalling 63-day momentum, and re-centring within the range. Per the standing instruction, when realised inflation runs hot while expectations stay anchored (or here, actively re-anchor lower), resolve toward the expectations driver rather than splitting the difference. The expectations read is not stale — it was formed at this meeting and is internally consistent (falsifier not yet triggered, decisive move well past noise threshold) — so it carries full weight rather than being discounted for staleness.

The labour cross-read reinforces the breakeven case rather than the inflation case: labor_tightness reports flat/inflecting (unemployment troughing then ticking up 0.3pp over three months), which undercuts the case for demand-pull inflation persistence and supports the idea that CPI's momentum shock is transitory rather than a signal of building wage-price pressure that would re-steepen expectations. This is exactly the "informed cross-read" the labor analyst offers into the inflation-persistence question, and it points the same direction as the expectations analyst: breakevens narrow.

